      "better_line": "Attack MiniMx Johnny at 17 life where the 2 damage is more meaningful and less likely to be blocked, or attack Qwen3 Contra at 10 life to pressure the lowest-life player",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 274,
      "player": "GPT5n Optim",
      "decision_index": 77,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Both modes of Silumgar's Command were used on the same target (Kestia, the Cultivator). The bounce (mode 2) already removes Kestia, making the -3/-3 (mode 3) completely wasted. This throws away half the value of a 5-mana spell.",
      "action_taken": "Targeted Kestia, the Cultivator with both the bounce and the -3/-3 modes of Silumgar's Command, wasting the -3/-3 effect.",
      "better_line": "Bounce Kestia with mode 2, and use mode 3 (-3/-3) to kill Lotus Cobra (2/1) or Skyhunter Skirmisher (1/1), removing an additional threat from the board.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 278,
      "player": "MiniMx Johnny",
      "decision_index": 81,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "MiniMx Johnny chose to bounce Rakdos Carnarium back to hand when it was the land that just entered and triggered the bounce ability. This means they effectively wasted their land drop — they played Rakdos Carnarium, then bounced it back to their hand, netting zero mana development and losing a turn's land drop. They should have bounced a different land (like the basic Island or Swamp) to keep Rakdos Carnarium on the battlefield, which would provide {B}{R} mana. Bouncing a basic land and replaying it next turn would have been far better for mana development. The player's reasoning about 'dealing 1 damage to finish Qwen3 Contra' makes no sense — Rakdos Carnarium doesn't deal damage.",
      "action_taken": "Bounced Rakdos Carnarium (the land that just entered) back to their own hand, completely wasting the land drop.",
      "better_line": "Bounce a less valuable land like Island or Swamp back to hand, keeping Rakdos Carnarium on the battlefield to produce {B}{R} mana. This preserves mana development while still allowing the bounced basic to be replayed next turn.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 283,
      "player": "MiniMx Johnny",
      "decision_index": 83,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Qwen3 Contra is at 4 life and has no blockers. GPT5n Optim has Lotus Cobra (2/1) which can attack Qwen3 Contra for lethal (or close to it). Meanwhile, MiniMx Johnny is at 13 life and has a Merchant of Secrets (1/1) that could block. Attacking MiniMx Johnny with just a 2/1 Lotus Cobra deals minimal damage (1 if blocked, 2 if not) and doesn't accomplish much. Attacking Qwen3 Contra would deal 2 damage, bringing them to 2 life and putting them in an extremely precarious position - essentially eliminating a player on the next attack. The aftermath text shows Angel of the Dire Hour appeared (likely flashed in by GPT4om Memoir) killing Lotus Cobra, and Outpost Siege dealt 4 damage to GPT4om Memoir when the creature left. But regardless of the Angel response, the correct strategic choice was to attack the 4-life player to either eliminate them or force them to spend resources defending.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked MiniMx Johnny (13 life) with Lotus Cobra",
      "better_line": "Attack Qwen3 Contra (4 life) with Lotus Cobra to deal 2 damage and bring them to 2 life, threatening elimination next turn",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 304,
      "player": "GPT5n Optim",
      "decision_index": 87,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "MiniMx Johnny played Island instead of Rakdos Carnarium. They have almost no red mana production (only Command Tower), yet their hand is full of red-requiring spells: Cauldron Dance ({4}{B}{R}), Etherium-Horn Sorcerer ({4}{U}{R}), and Mairsil ({1}{U}{B}{R}). Playing Rakdos Carnarium would provide a crucial second red source for future turns.",
      "action_taken": "Played Island, adding a redundant blue mana source",
      "better_line": "Play Rakdos Carnarium (bouncing Island or Swamp) to gain access to red mana, enabling future casting of Cauldron Dance, Etherium-Horn Sorcerer, or Mairsil from the single additional red source",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 312,
      "player": "MiniMx Johnny",
      "decision_index": 92,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "MiniMx Johnny attacked with only Merchant of Secrets (1 damage) instead of all creatures at Qwen3 Contra who is at 4 HP with no blockers. Attacking with all available creatures (Merchant of Secrets 1/1 + Mercurial Chemister 2/3) would deal 3 damage total, leaving Qwen3 at 1 HP instead of 3 HP, making them trivially easy to finish off.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with only Merchant of Secrets for 1 damage against the 4 HP player",
      "better_line": "Choose 'All attack' to send both Merchant of Secrets and Mercurial Chemister at Qwen3 Contra for 3 total damage, leaving them at 1 HP. The 2 extra damage toward eliminating a player is worth more than drawing 2 cards with Chemister's tap ability.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 314,
      "player": "MiniMx Johnny",
      "decision_index": 92,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "MiniMx Johnny attacked Qwen3 Contra (at 4 life, no blockers) with only Merchant of Secrets (1/1) for 1 damage, when attacking with both Merchant of Secrets (1/1) and Mercurial Chemister (2/3) would deal 3 damage. Furthermore, activating Mirror of the Forebears for {1} to copy either Wizard and attacking with all three creatures would deal 4-5 damage, which is lethal against Qwen3 Contra's 4 life.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with only Merchant of Secrets (1/1) dealing 1 damage to Qwen3 Contra at 4 life.",
      "better_line": "Attack with Merchant of Secrets, Mercurial Chemister, and activate Mirror of the Forebears to copy a Wizard and attack with it as well, dealing lethal damage (4+ damage) to eliminate Qwen3 Contra.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 314,
      "player": "MiniMx Johnny",
      "decision_index": 92,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "GPT5n Optim had Gratuitous Violence in hand (costs 5 mana) and approximately 10+ mana available. Casting it before combat would double all combat damage: Bello (3→6), Outpost Siege (4→8), Bootleggers' Stash (4→8) = 22 total damage. This would allow splitting attacks to kill both MiniMx Johnny (7 life) and GPT4om Memoir (10 life) in one combat, winning the game outright. Without it, they can only kill one opponent this turn.",
      "action_taken": "Went straight to combat with 'All attack' without casting Gratuitous Violence first",
      "better_line": "Cast Gratuitous Violence (5 mana) in precombat main phase, then attack with all three creatures split between Johnny and Memoir for lethal on both opponents, winning the game immediately",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 332,
      "player": "GPT5n Optim",
      "decision_index": 95,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Attacking MiniMx Johnny means Bello (3/3) gets blocked by Mercurial Chemister (2/3), losing the combat damage card draw trigger from Bello and taking 2 damage on Bello. Attacking GPT4om Memoir instead would likely result in both Outpost Siege and Bello being unblocked (Memoir's blockers are less favorable), dealing 7 total damage (Memoir to 3), drawing a card from Bello's trigger, and keeping Bello at full health. Both targets end at 3 life, but attacking Memoir provides strictly more value.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked MiniMx Johnny with both creatures, resulting in Bello being blocked by Mercurial Chemister",
      "better_line": "Attack GPT4om Memoir with both Outpost Siege and Bello for likely 7 unblocked damage, getting a card draw from Bello's combat damage trigger while preserving Bello's toughness",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 333,
      "player": "GPT5n Optim",
      "decision_index": 95,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded Gratuitous Violence (a castable enchantment that doubles creature damage, very relevant with opponent at 10 life) while keeping Decimate, which requires targets for artifact, creature, enchantment, AND land simultaneously - and the sole remaining opponent (GPT4om Memoir) has no artifacts or enchantments on board, making Decimate uncastable.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Gratuitous Violence as one of the 3 discards",
      "better_line": "Should have discarded Decimate instead of Gratuitous Violence, since Decimate cannot currently be cast (no valid artifact/enchantment targets on opponent's board) and Gratuitous Violence would double damage output against the last opponent at 10 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 338,
      "player": "GPT5n Optim",
      "decision_index": 96,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "At 3 life, casting Chaos Warp before playing a land drop is a minor sequencing error. While they have sufficient mana already, playing a Mountain first would leave more mana available after Chaos Warp resolves for potential follow-up plays like Teapot Slinger.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Chaos Warp before making a land drop",
      "better_line": "Play Mountain first, then cast Chaos Warp targeting the most dangerous threat (likely Skyhunter Skirmisher or Jazal Goldmane), preserving maximum mana flexibility for the rest of the turn",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 351,
      "player": "GPT5n Optim",
      "decision_index": 100,
    }
  ],
